<section class="w3-services" id="services" aria-labelledby="w3-services-title">
  <div class="w3-services-inner">
    <header class="w3-services-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="w3-services-kicker">LEAK DETECTION SERVICES <span>·</span> HOUSTON, TX</p>
        <h2 id="w3-services-title">Our Services</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Explore common leak locations, the signs people notice, and the details that can help guide an inspection.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="w3-services-grid">
      <article class="w3-service-card">
        <figure class="w3-service-image"><img src="/images/website-2/houston-leak-inspection-hero.png" alt="Technician using acoustic equipment to inspect a home floor" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        <div class="w3-service-copy">
          <p class="w3-service-type">01 <span>UNDER THE FLOOR</span></p>
          <h3>Slab leak detection</h3>
          <p>A leak in a water line beneath a concrete slab can be hard to spot. A warm patch, damp flooring, or a meter that moves when water is off may be clues. An inspection helps narrow down whether a buried line could be involved.</p>
          <p class="w3-service-clues"><strong>Common clues</strong> Warm floor · damp edges · unexplained water use</p>
          <details class="w3-service-details"><summary><span>Signs &amp; inspection notes</span><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="w3-service-detail-copy"><strong>Useful to share</strong><p>Where the floor feels warm or damp, when you first noticed it, and whether the meter moves when fixtures are off. Those details help focus an inspection; they do not confirm a slab leak.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-service-card">
        <figure class="w3-service-image"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/8624464/pexels-photo-8624464.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="Water staining on a bedroom ceiling" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        <div class="w3-service-copy">
          <p class="w3-service-type">02 <span>BEHIND WALLS &amp; CEILINGS</span></p>
          <h3>Wall &amp; ceiling leak detection</h3>
          <p>A recurring stain, peeling paint, or a musty smell may come from plumbing—but roof leaks and condensation can look similar. Where the spot appears and when it changes can help narrow down the likely source.</p>
          <p class="w3-service-clues"><strong>Common clues</strong> Water stains · bubbling paint · recurring dampness</p>
          <details class="w3-service-details"><summary><span>Signs &amp; inspection notes</span><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="w3-service-detail-copy"><strong>Useful to share</strong><p>Take a photo and note whether the spot changes after rain, a shower, or other fixture use. A stain can show where moisture appears without showing where it started.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-service-card">
        <figure class="w3-service-image"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4406597/pexels-photo-4406597.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="Water leaking from a pipe above a grassy yard" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        <div class="w3-service-copy">
          <p class="w3-service-type">03 <span>OUTSIDE THE HOME</span></p>
          <h3>Underground water line detection</h3>
          <p>A soggy strip, unexplained meter movement, or a drop in pressure can be clues to a buried supply line issue. Rain, irrigation, and drainage can cause similar signs, so note when and where the wet area returns.</p>
          <p class="w3-service-clues"><strong>Common clues</strong> Persistent wet ground · meter movement · pressure change</p>
          <details class="w3-service-details"><summary><span>Signs &amp; inspection notes</span><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="w3-service-detail-copy"><strong>Useful to share</strong><p>Note recent rain, irrigation times, where the ground stays wet, and any meter movement when water is not being used. Drainage and irrigation can create similar patterns.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="w3-service-card">
        <figure class="w3-service-image"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/16509869/pexels-photo-16509869.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="Technician working with commercial plumbing pipes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        <div class="w3-service-copy">
          <p class="w3-service-type">04 <span>BUSINESSES &amp; BUILDINGS</span></p>
          <h3>Commercial leak detection</h3>
          <p>In a larger property, leaks may affect more than one room or interrupt normal use. Sharing which areas are affected, when the signs occur, and any changes in water use helps focus the inspection.</p>
          <p class="w3-service-clues"><strong>Useful details</strong> Affected areas · timing · changes in water use</p>
          <details class="w3-service-details"><summary><span>Planning &amp; inspection notes</span><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div class="w3-service-detail-copy"><strong>Before the visit</strong><p>List the affected rooms or floors, when signs appear, and any access or operating-hour requirements. Larger plumbing layouts can change what an inspection needs to cover.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

